WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.933
Feature selection.

2
00:00:02.233 --> 00:00:05.477
Every useless column is noise your model can overfit on.

3
00:00:05.477 --> 00:00:06.867
Let's learn to cut them.

4
00:00:08.067 --> 00:00:11.933
Feature selection drops columns that add noise instead of signal.

5
00:00:12.233 --> 00:00:15.533
Fewer columns means faster training and cheaper inference.

6
00:00:15.833 --> 00:00:17.833
It also means less to overfit on.

7
00:00:18.133 --> 00:00:20.933
And a shorter explanation to give your stakeholders.

8
00:00:22.133 --> 00:00:25.200
There are three families of methods, in increasing cost.

9
00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:27.759
Filters score each feature on its own.

10
00:00:27.759 --> 00:00:30.433
They are cheap, but they ignore interactions.

11
00:00:30.733 --> 00:00:33.819
Wrappers train on many subsets and keep the best.

12
00:00:33.819 --> 00:00:36.400
Expensive, but they respect interactions.

13
00:00:36.700 --> 00:00:43.533
Embedded methods let the model choose while it trains, like tree importances or L1 coefficients pushed to zero.

14
00:00:44.733 --> 00:00:46.467
In practice you run a funnel.

15
00:00:46.767 --> 00:00:48.167
Start with every feature.

16
00:00:48.467 --> 00:00:49.967
Rank them by importance.

17
00:00:50.267 --> 00:00:51.800
Keep only the top slice.

18
00:00:52.100 --> 00:00:53.314
Retrain and compare.

19
00:00:53.314 --> 00:00:55.500
Often the score doesn't drop at all.

20
00:00:56.700 --> 00:00:59.467
Here is the embedded approach with a random forest.

21
00:00:59.767 --> 00:01:02.100
First we train a forest on every feature.

22
00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:05.967
Then we rank the columns by importance and keep the top five.

23
00:01:06.267 --> 00:01:09.867
Finally we retrain on just those five and compare the scores.

24
00:01:11.067 --> 00:01:13.133
Selection is not only about speed.

25
00:01:13.433 --> 00:01:17.692
On the exoplanet classifier, catalog version lands in the top three.

26
00:01:17.692 --> 00:01:21.200
That is suspicious, since it says nothing about planets.

27
00:01:21.500 --> 00:01:26.767
It turns out confirmed planets were re-catalogued later, so the version number leaks the answer.

28
00:01:27.067 --> 00:01:30.867
Reading the importance ranking is how you catch a leak before it ships.

29
00:01:32.067 --> 00:01:32.875
Let's recap.

30
00:01:32.875 --> 00:01:35.500
Selection drops columns that add noise.

31
00:01:35.800 --> 00:01:39.600
Filter, wrapper and embedded methods go from cheap to costly.

32
00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:42.233
Rank, keep the top slice, and retrain.

33
00:01:42.533 --> 00:01:44.750
And importances can expose leaks.

34
00:01:44.750 --> 00:01:46.900
Next up: missing value handling.
